{"canonical_record":{"canonical_payload_sha256":"adae3ddc1ad36783af2ba8586451bb95d25a5380abdd44ac8bdcb67bf1622aa7","full_review_record_id":"fullrev_f6f165dd7ba4bdf13859661bf99015d29a13f0b29602afc4f3e39f07da2ad191","overall_assessment":"The paper presents a novel experimental framework for evaluating out-of-distribution novelty generation by holding out entire classes, which is a distinct contribution from standard in-distribution generative modeling. It introduces specific metrics (out-of-class count, max, objectness, Parzen) and demonstrates their utility through a large-scale study of autoencoders and GANs. The work provides a clear conceptual distinction between probabilistic and constructive generative models, arguing that the latter is more suitable for novelty generation. The experimental setup is well-defined, and the analysis of metric correlations offers insights into the trade-offs between different evaluation criteria. The paper effectively positions itself against prior work in computational creativity and generative modeling, highlighting the need for new evaluation principles in the context of out-of-distribution generation.","paper_id":"ByEPMj5el","paper_identity_sha256":"31fbadfb27614dca30c86ec4efe9d439a57eafe529ad80e7eb5a8e4c322ff304","prompt_identity_sha256":"be2f493f1070a0a9065a53091188ea160729cfb810bd4daf7e6b3b8328ffdc57","raw_response_sha256":"5d3c2da4a18a484a3e313816a868df27e3a686454e4a184aa7d3b8273c625041","request_identity_sha256":"1b881cff4bb9e6842d0637e9d79a2ebb86b2e3b7718ad395e9d50bfd2db877a7","reviewer_identity_sha256":"32b29c26fbf1508e066dfb25b1a9a3fe0654c5869a7da3020e1e8e61940befe3","role":"ORIG","schema_version":"agentreview-full-review-corpus-v4","structural_context_sha256":"b1a57423ed0ad7f6034f1e0658bacac795fd735d74a1c49c22e1072de0235c87","subdimension_sections":[{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-002","SRC-007","SRC-023"],"review_text":"The paper introduces a novel problem formulation by defining out-of-distribution novelty generation as the task of generating objects from classes unknown at training time. This is a significant departure from traditional generative modeling, which focuses on in-distribution generation. The authors argue that this new problem formulation is necessary to study creativity in machine learning, as it allows for the generation of previously unknown but meaningful objects. The problem is well-motivated by the limitations of existing generative models, which are designed to eliminate out-of-distribution sampling. The paper provides a clear and actionable definition of creativity as the generation of out-of-distribution novelty, which is a creative and meaningful new problem formulation that opens new avenues for research.","subdimension_id":"ORIG-PROB"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-013","SRC-014","SRC-015","SRC-016","SRC-017","SRC-018","SRC-019"],"review_text":"The paper does not introduce a new algorithmic approach or architectural design but rather re-evaluates existing architectures (autoencoders and GANs) under the new goal of out-of-distribution novelty generation. The authors argue that the probabilistic generative framework is too restrictive for studying novelty generation and propose a constructive approach where the generator is designed to produce sample objects similar to elements of the training data but not identical to them. This conceptual shift is a significant contribution, as it highlights the limitations of the probabilistic framework and suggests a new direction for research. However, the paper does not introduce a new algorithm or architecture, so the methodological contribution is more conceptual than technical.","subdimension_id":"ORIG-MTH"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-021","SRC-022","SRC-024","SRC-025","SRC-026","SRC-027","SRC-039","SRC-040"],"review_text":"The paper provides a novel analysis of evaluation metrics for out-of-distribution novelty generation. The authors review and analyze the most common evaluation techniques from the point of view of measuring out-of-distribution novelty and argue that likelihood-based techniques inherently limit exploration and novelty generation. They carefully select a couple of measures (out-of-class count, max, objectness, Parzen) and demonstrate their applicability for out-of-distribution novelty detection in experiments. The analysis of inter-score correlations and human annotation counts provides insights into the trade-offs between different evaluation criteria. The finding that out-of-class Parzen is especially bad at picking good out-of-class generators is a novel insight that sheds light on the limitations of existing metrics. The paper also introduces a new experimental setup for assessing out-of-distribution novelty by out-of-class scoring, which is a significant contribution to the field.","subdimension_id":"ORIG-ANL"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-030","SRC-031","SRC-032","SRC-033","SRC-034","SRC-035","SRC-036"],"review_text":"The paper proposes a new experimental setup for evaluating out-of-distribution novelty generation. The authors hold out entire classes for evaluating the generative potential of models, which is a novel approach compared to traditional held-out validation. The experimental setup is well-defined, with detailed descriptions of the autoencoder and GAN architectures, hyperparameters, and generation procedures. The authors run a large-scale experimentation to study the ability of novelty generation of a wide set of different autoencoders and GANs, which is a significant contribution to the field. The experimental setup is not derivative and adds value beyond existing benchmarks by providing a new way to evaluate generative models for out-of-distribution novelty generation.","subdimension_id":"ORIG-EXP"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-018","SRC-019","SRC-022","SRC-024","SRC-025","SRC-026"],"review_text":"The paper combines existing techniques (autoencoders, GANs, discriminators) in a novel way to evaluate out-of-distribution novelty generation. The authors use discriminators to assess the quality of generators whose very goal is to generate novelty: objects that are distinguishable from existing objects. This combination is well-justified, as it allows for the evaluation of out-of-distribution novelty without relying on likelihood-based metrics. The paper also introduces new metrics (out-of-class count, max, objectness, Parzen) that are specifically designed for out-of-distribution novelty generation. The combination of existing techniques and new metrics is a creative and compelling contribution to the field.","subdimension_id":"ORIG-COM"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-036","SRC-037","SRC-038","SRC-039","SRC-040"],"review_text":"The paper provides a critical assessment of existing generative models and evaluation metrics in the context of out-of-distribution novelty generation. The authors find that tuning generative models for in-distribution generation will make them 'memorize' the classes they are trained to sample from, which is a significant limitation for out-of-distribution novelty generation. They also find that the vast majority of architectures designed and tuned in the literature are not generating out-of-class novelty naturally. The analysis of inter-score correlations and human annotation counts provides a critical assessment of the quality of the defined metrics in evaluating out-of-distribution generators. The finding that out-of-class Parzen is especially bad at picking good out-of-class generators is a negative result that challenges the assumption that likelihood-based metrics are suitable for out-of-distribution novelty generation. The paper's critical assessment is thoughtful and well-justified, providing valuable insights into the limitations of existing methods.","subdimension_id":"ORIG-NEG"}]},"disposition":"CANONICAL_FULL_REVIEW_COMMITTED","item_commit_sha256":"15c9b1a6cab2ed0e5a4afa43bd42b73726670793786564856b5bdf956d049ec1","paper_id":"ByEPMj5el","paper_ordinal":67,"raw_attempts":[{"assistant_content_base64":"","assistant_content_sha256":"5d3c2da4a18a484a3e313816a868df27e3a686454e4a184aa7d3b8273c625041","attempt_index":0,"error":"","finish_reason":"stop","paper_id":"ByEPMj5el","raw_provider_response_base64":"","raw_provider_response_sha256":"ad89358bb3a71075247f6ef7f186bd2c79d69dc342abd17d25b6e0ff2eb95ffe","request_identity_sha256":"1b881cff4bb9e6842d0637e9d79a2ebb86b2e3b7718ad395e9d50bfd2db877a7","role":"ORIG","schema_version":"no-memory-full-review-corpus-build-v6","usage":{"completion_tokens":1651,"prompt_tokens":17736,"prompt_tokens_details":null,"total_tokens":19387},"valid":true}],"request_identity_sha256":"1b881cff4bb9e6842d0637e9d79a2ebb86b2e3b7718ad395e9d50bfd2db877a7","role":"ORIG","role_ordinal":3,"schema_version":"corpus-build-bootstrap-checkpoint-v4"}